Title: Married and Yet Not Married
Order: 6
Production Code: 1x06

FADE IN:

EXT. NORFOLK - CAMPOSTELLA BRIDGE - EVENING (SPRING)

Bernard is at the very acme of fame, the most noted negro of his day. He has invited Viola Martin to go buggy riding. They have driven out of Norfolk over Campostella bridge and far into the country, past farm hands preparing the soil for seed sowing. About eight o'clock they are returning. Bernard has determined to know his fate before he reaches Viola's home.

Midway the bridge he pulls his reins and the horse stands still. The dark waters of the small river sweep on beneath them. Night has just begun to spread out her sombre wings, bedecked with silent stars. A bend in the river brings a shore directly facing them; a green lawn runs back into the shadows of evening. Amid a group of trees stands a little hut that looks the hut of an old widower, neglected, forsaken, sad.

BERNARD
Viola, I feel to-night that all my honors are empty. They feel to me like a load crushing me down rather than a pedestal raising me up. I am not happy. I long for the solitude of those trees. That decaying old house calls eloquently unto something within me. How I would like to enter there and lay me down to sleep, free from the cares and divested of the gewgaws of the world.

VIOLA MARTIN
Ah, Mr. Belgrave, something terrible is gnawing at your heart for one so young, so brilliant, so prosperous as you are to talk thus. Make a confidante of me and let me help to remove the load, if I can.

BERNARD
I have gone forth into life to win certain honors and snatch from fame a wreath, and now that I have succeeded, I behold this evening, as never before, that it is not worthy of the purpose for which I designed it. My work is all in vain.

VIOLA MARTIN
Mr. Belgrave, you must not talk so sadly.

Bernard turns and suddenly grasps her hands.

BERNARD
Viola, I love you. I have nothing to offer you worthy of you. I can find nothing worthy, attain nothing worthy. I love you to desperation. Will you give yourself to a wretch like me? Say no! don't throw away your beauty, your love on so common a piece of clay.

Viola utters a loud, piercing scream. The horse bolts across the bridge. Bernard fights for the reins. When he gets them, Viola has fainted. The cool air revives her; she awakes trembling violently and begs him to take her home at once. He does, much puzzled.

INT. BERNARD'S ROOM - NIGHT

He does not sleep. He paces up and down, glancing at the clock every five minutes. He raises the window and strains his eyes for any sign of dawn.

INT. MARTIN HOUSE - PARLOR - MORNING

As soon as he thinks Miss Martin astir, he is at the door. They each look sad and forlorn. Viola catches hold of his hand and leads him silently into the parlor. He sits on the divan and she sits close by his side. She turns her face up to his.

VIOLA MARTIN
(whispering)
Kiss me, Bernard.

He seizes her and kisses her rapturously. She rises and sits in a chair facing him, at a distance.

VIOLA MARTIN (CONT'D)
(calmly, almost icily)
Bernard, you know that I love you. It was I that asked you to kiss me. Always remember that. But as much as I love you I shall never be your wife. Never, never.

BERNARD
Oh God! Is all my labor in vain, my honors common dirt, my future one dreary waste? Shall I lose that which has been an ever shining, never setting sun to me? Viola! If you love me you shall be my wife.

VIOLA MARTIN
A power higher than either you or I has decreed it otherwise.

BERNARD
Who is he? Tell me who he is that dare separate us and I swear I will kill him.

VIOLA MARTIN
Would you kill God?

Bernard returns to his seat and sits down suddenly.

BERNARD
Viola Martin, you are making a fool of me. Tell me plainly why we cannot be man and wife, if you love me as you say you do?

VIOLA MARTIN
Bernard, call here to-morrow at 10 o'clock and I will tell you all. If you can then remove my objections all will be well.

He leaps up, eager to get away. He goes home and spends the day perusing his text-book on logic, conjuring imaginary objections and demolishing them.

INT. MARTIN HOUSE - DINING ROOM - DAY

When Bernard has gone Viola throws herself prostrate on the floor, moaning and sobbing. After a while she rises and goes to the dining room door. Mrs. Martin sits quietly sewing.

VIOLA MARTIN
Mamma, I shall be busy writing all day in my room. Let no one disturb me.

Her mother assures her no one shall.

INT. VIOLA'S ROOM - DAY

The beauty spot of the house: pictures of her own painting on the walls, articles of fancy work, flowers and roses. She opens her tiny writing desk and writes a letter to her father, one to her mother and one to Bernard. The letter to Bernard is torn up and re-written time and again; fast falling tears spoil it as fast as she writes.

EXT. MARTIN HOUSE - REAR PORCH - EVENTIDE

Viola and her mother watch the sun glide out of sight without a struggle, without a murmur. Viola's eye lingers long on the spot. She draws a heavy sigh, catches her mother round the waist and leads her into the parlor.

INT. MARTIN HOUSE - PARLOR - NIGHT

Viola becomes all gayety, but her mother can see that it is forced. She plays the piano and sings, her rich soprano swelling full and round. Passers-by pause to listen and pass on feeling sad at heart.

MRS. MARTIN
(to herself)
What did it mean?

Viola's father comes from the post-office, where he is a clerk. Viola runs to him joyously, pulls him into the parlor, sits on his knee stroking his chin and nestling her head on his bosom. She makes him tell her tales as he did when she was a child. She laughs, but not with her accustomed clear, golden ring.

She kisses them good night. At the head of the stairway she returns and without a word kisses her parents again.

INT. VIOLA'S ROOM - NIGHT

She fastens the door. She takes the three letters and places them on the floor a few inches apart where they will attract immediate attention. She lies down on her bed and puts one arm across her bosom. With her other hand she turns on the gas jet by the head of her bed. She places that hand across her bosom as well.

EXT. MARTIN HOUSE - NIGHT

The moon rises and sheds its sad, quiet light through the half turned shutters.

SUPER: "THUS IN THIS FORBIDDEN PATH VIOLA MARTIN HAD GONE TO HIM WHO SAID: 'COME UNTO ME ALL YE THAT LABOR AND ARE HEAVY LADEN, AND I WILL GIVE YOU REST.'"

INT. MARTIN HOUSE - PARLOR - TEN O'CLOCK THE NEXT DAY

Mrs. Martin invites him in and informs him that Viola has not arisen. Bernard says Viola made an engagement for ten o'clock. Mrs. Martin looks alarmed: Viola is invariably punctual.

She leaves hurriedly. Her knees smite together as she fancies she discovers the scent of escaping gas. She clings to the banisters and drags her way to Viola's door; the smell is unmistakable; she falls forward, uttering a loud scream.

Bernard dashes up the stairs, rushes past Mrs. Martin and bursts open the door. He draws back aghast. He seizes her lifeless form, beautiful in death, and smothers those silent lips with kisses.

INT. VIOLA'S ROOM - LATER

Mrs. Martin rushes in, sees her child dead, utters piercing shrieks, falls to the floor in a swoon. Bernard lays Viola on her bed tenderly and sets about restoring Mrs. Martin. The room is full of anxious neighbors.

INT. VIOLA'S ROOM - DAY

The three letters.

INSERT - THE LETTER TO MAMA

VIOLA (V.O.)
My dear, dear, heart-broken Mama: I am in the hands of God. Whatever He does is just, is right, is the only thing to be done. Knowing this, do not grieve after me. Take poor Bernard for your son and love him as you did me. I make that as my sole dying request of you. One long sweet clinging kiss ere I drop into the ocean of death to be lost in its tossing waves. Viola.

INSERT - THE LETTER TO PAPA

VIOLA (V.O.)
Beloved Papa: Your little daughter is gone. Her heart, though torn, bleeding, dead, gave, as it were, an after throb of pain as it thought of you. In life you never denied me a request. I have one to make from my grave, knowing that you will not deny me. Love Bernard as your son; draw him to you, so that, when in your old age you go tottering to your tomb in quest of me, you may have a son to bear you up. Take my lifeless body on your knee and kiss me as you did of old. It will help me to rest sweetly in my grave. Your little Vie.

INT. VIOLA'S ROOM - THE LETTER TO BERNARD

Viola's Letter. Bernard reads it.

VIOLA (V.O.)
Dear Bernard: Viola has loved and left you. Unto you, above all others, I owe a full explanation of the deed which I have committed; and I shall therefore lay bare my heart to you. My father was a colonel in the Civil War and when I was very young he would make my little heart thrill with patriotic fervor as he told me of the deeds of daring of the gallant Negro soldiers. As a result, when nothing but a tiny girl, I determined to be a heroine and find some outlet for my patriotic feeling. This became a consuming passion. Just two years prior to my meeting you, a book entitled, "White Supremacy and Negro Subordination," by the merest accident came into my possession. That book made a revelation to me of a most startling nature.

VIOLA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
While I lived I could not tell you what I am about to tell you. Death has brought me the privilege. That book proved to me that the intermingling of the races in sexual relationship was sapping the vitality of the Negro race and, in fact, was slowly but surely exterminating the race. It demonstrated that the fourth generation of the children born of intermarrying mulattoes were invariably sterile or woefully lacking in vital force. It asserted that only in the most rare instances were children born of this fourth generation and in no case did such children reach maturity.

VIOLA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
While this intermingling was impairing the vital force of our race and exterminating it, it was having no such effect on the white race for the following reason. Every half-breed, or for that, every person having a tinge of Negro blood, the white people cast off. We receive the cast off with open arms and he comes to us with his devitalizing power. Thus, the white man was slowly exterminating us and our total extinction was but a short period of time distant.

VIOLA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I determined to spend my life fighting the evil. My first step was to solemnly pledge God to never marry a mulatto man. My next resolve was to part in every honorable way all courting couples of mulatto people that I could. My other and greatest task was to persuade the evil women of my race to cease their criminal conduct with white men and I went about pleading with them upon my knees to desist. Many had faith in me and I was the means of redeeming numbers of these erring ones.

VIOLA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
When you came, I loved you. I struggled hard against that love. God, alone, knows how I battled against it. Sometimes I would appear indifferent to you with the hope of driving you away, but then my love would come surging with all the more violence and sweep me from my feet. At last, you seemed to draw away from me and I was happy. But you at last proposed to me when I thought all such notions were dead. At once I foresaw my tragic end.

VIOLA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Bernard, how happy would I have been, how deliriously happy, could I but have stood beside you at the altar and sworn fidelity to you. Ours would have been an ideal home. But it was not to be. I had to choose between you and my race. Your noble heart, in its sober moments will sanction my choice. I would not have died if I could have lived without proving false to my race.

VIOLA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Dear Bernard, I have a favor to ask of you. Secure the book of which I spoke to you. Study the question of the intermingling of the races. If miscegenation is in reality destroying us, dedicate your soul to the work of separating the white and colored races. Do not let them intermingle. Erect moral barriers to separate them. If you fail in this, make the separation physical; lead our people forth from this accursed land. Do this and I shall not have died in vain. Visit my grave now and then to drop thereon a flower and a flag, but no tears. If in the shadowy beyond, whose mists I feel gathering about me, there is a place where kindred spirits meet, you and I shall surely meet again. Though I could not in life, I will in death sign myself, your loving wife, Viola Belgrave.

INT. MARTIN HOUSE - HALLWAY - DAY

Bernard emerges from the room where his sun has set to rise no more. His eyes flash, his nostrils dilate, his bosom heaves. He lifts his proud head and turns his face so that the light of the sky may fall full upon it. Lifting up his hands, trembling with emotion as though supplicating for the strength of a god:

BERNARD
By the eternal heavens these abominable horrors shall cease. The races, whose union has been fraught with every curse known to earth and hell, must separate. Viola demands it and Bernard obeys.

INT. BERNARD'S BOARDING HOUSE - DAY

With his hands thrust into his pockets and his hat pulled over his grief-stricken eyes, Bernard slowly wends his way to his boarding place. He locks himself in his room and denies himself to all callers. He paces to and fro; he sits and leans his head upon the bed. His landlady knocks and calls through the key hole that there is a telegram. He signs for and receives it, tears it open and reads.

INSERT - THE TELEGRAM

"Waco, Texas. HON. BERNARD BELGRAVE, M.C. Come to Waco at once. If you fail to come you will make the mistake of your life. Come. BELTON PIEDMONT."

BERNARD
Yes, I'll go. Anywhere, for anything.

He seems grateful for something to divert his thoughts. He sends Viola's family a note truthfully stating that he is unequal to the task of attending Viola's funeral, and that for his part she is not dead and never shall be. The parents have read Bernard's letter left by Viola and know the whole story; they too feel it is best for Bernard to go.

EXT. NORFOLK - RAILWAY STATION - AFTERNOON

Bernard takes the train that afternoon. A journey of four days.

CUT TO:
